## What was missing and what I did

The disclosure code itself had already passed all three other checks; the commit simply never touched CLAUDE.md, despite it being named in the task's Files line. I shipped a user-visible behaviour change (a new stderr line) with no entry in the file re-injected as authoritative context every session.

**I did not just transcribe the task file's plan into prose.** I read the landed code first, and that turned up a correction worth more than the paragraph:

• **The task said "five instruction files". The const has six.** `PROJECT_CONTEXT_FILES` includes `.yoyo/instructions.md`, which neither the advisory, the task file, nor the existing CLAUDE.md bullet named. The bullet was a hand-typed copy of a const, one entry short, in the authoritative file — and the task file inherited the same miscount from the same place, so the error propagated into the plan before anyone read the const. That is recorded as a superseded enumeration rather than silently fixed, with a pointer to count it in the code.

Claims I verified rather than asserted:

• #902 — confirmed OPEN with the exact title (the prior attempt filed it; I referenced it rather than re-filing).
• The `--safe-mode` hatch — read at src/cli.rs:2450-2451. It works, and --restricted implies it, which is a second hatch the note does not name. Naming an unverified hatch would have been a confident wrong claim.
• No second filename list — the note derives names from PROJECT_CONTEXT_FILES at the load loop.
• No register line owed — src/context.rs is 1219 lines and absent from the register. My first grep suggested "6 warnings"; reading the matched lines showed they were test names. I checked before claiming zero.
• Positive control, run rather than assumed — neutering project_instruction_note reddens exactly 2 tests, both new, no pre-existing test moved, restored to a byte-empty diff with 0 markers.

One place I made the write-up weaker than the task's language: the task called test 3 a byte-identity guard. The landed test asserts absence of the note's wording, not a whole-string assert_eq! against a captured baseline. Under the positive control, both surviving new tests assert absence — which a dead branch satisfies identically — so they are boundary pins, structurally incapable of failing under that control, not evidence the pass-through works. I wrote that down rather than letting "2 guards held" stand.

